matthew taylor um  should nhs employees be required to be  vaccinated as a condition of employment  uh i think that that is not necessary um  the overwhelming majority of nhs staff  are choosing to be vaccinated  and the important thing is to support  people give people the opportunity to be  vaccinated  so  i think we would want to say that there  is no  necessity for uh compulsion  kind of surveillance of people at this  stage because  the  staff themselves are doing the right  thing  so you wouldn't uh be putting any energy  into  consulting on that as the  minister told us he was going to do  earlier on  i think if we felt that there was a a  real issue  there then  um you know it is something that needs  to be addressed of course health service  workers like anybody else need to take  responsibility for the decisions they  make and the implications they have for  other people but you know i i think that  what we should focus on is the fact that  overwhelmingly nhs staff  are doing the right thing and indeed you  know working beyond their hours and have  been dealing now for you know 18 months  with  a national crisis and dealing with it uh  heroically so  you know i think the question is not  are there some nhs staff who aren't  vaccinated the question is how do we  support  those nhs staff who have been  doing what we need them to do through  this crisis  let's look at  sorry let's look at what's got what's uh  in prospect if the government decides to  uh vaccinate 12 to 15 year olds uh or to  offer booster jab to  older people do you think the system is  resilient enough to cope with that  demand  well we've  seen heroic efforts in terms of  vaccination  and that continues  and there are issues about  supply their issues about capacity and  clearly the government has to make  decisions in terms of who it is that it  prioritizes in relation to this and  these are difficult choices nobody  pretends that they're not  in terms of making a booster um  available to people like myself or the  people who've had two vaccinations and  also to  younger people because of the importance  of ensuring not just trying to minimize  infection rates but also to ensure that  young people are able to carry on in  education  so you know the health service stands  ready as it has done over the last 18  months if given the resources  to lead this national vaccination  program which has so far been a major uh  success but  you know trevor this is part of the the  question of living with uh covid and you  know the first  part of the vaccination program was to  get people vaccinated  and now we have to think about what is  required in terms of boosters also in  terms of  flu  vaccination so  these are hard choices it's important  that the government is open about those  choices and takes the best advice that  it can in terms of who it is we should  be prioritizing  you published a report this week which  to be honest described a system which is  pretty much on the edge you uh your  report said that covert 19 had had a  seismic impact unlike anything the nhs  has faced in its history and it to  describe the last two months as winter  in summer  do you believe that you've got the beds  and the people the trained staff to get  us through to next spring  yeah it's tough and you're absolutely  right trevor that  the leaders or members of our  organization tell us that  it feels and we're just coming out of  summer that when it feels like a  midwinter crisis all parts of the nhs  not just hospitals the ambulance service  gps community mental health services we  are running on hot  and you can't do that forever and so  that's one reason why it's incredibly  important to get some clarity from the  government  um about the funding for the rest of  this year that we sustain uh that  funding and that we have the funding  that we need on the assumption that the  pressures of covid are going to continue  and that's what it looks like is going  to happen so the two big challenges for  the health service are  funding the ongoing costs of covid and  that's the patients in hospital over  7000 in hospital right now ppe  um the pressure on mental health  services long hovid which is a major  issue and then secondly the backlog we  have  as we know well over five million people  on waiting lists the secretary of state  himself has said that he thinks that  that vehicle rise uh a great deal more  and we need to fund that care as well  now yes there are major issues about  workforce there are issues about uh the  kind of capacity that we have but  it is important to say that if you give  the health service the resources that it  needs it does the job  and even in these last few months while  we're still coping with covid we have  seen substantial progress in relation to  those people who've been waiting the  longest time  and we've seen progress also in other  critical areas like making sure that we  are checking people for the danger of a  cancer diagnosis so  despite these pressures where the nhs  has the resources it makes a difference  but we need that resourcing to to  continue  firstly for the rest of this year and  then for the years ahead
 

